  "response": "**Setup**\n\n- P(condition) = 1/500 = 0.002\n- P(no condition) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity = P(+|condition) = 0.98\n- Specificity = P(–|no condition) = 0.95 → false positive rate = 0.05\n\n**Bayes' theorem**\n\nP(+) = P(condition)·Sensitivity + P(no condition)·(1 – Specificity)\nP(+) = (0.002)(0.98) + (0.998)(0.05)\nP(+) = 0.00196 + 0.04990 = 0.05186\n\nP(condition | +) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794 → **3.78%**\n\nSo even with a positive test result, there's only about a 3.78% chance this person actually has the condition — a classic illustration of how a low base rate dominates the outcome despite a fairly accurate test.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
